{"course": {"schema_version": "1.0", "course_id": "mitocw-6-336j-fall-2003", "institution": "Massachusetts Institute of Technology", "platform": "MIT OpenCourseWare", "course_codes": ["6.336J"], "cross_listed_codes": ["2.096J", "16.910J"], "course_title": "Introduction to Numerical Simulation (SMA 5211)", "course_slug": "6-336j-introduction-to-numerical-simulation-sma-5211-fall-2003", "term": "Fall", "year": 2003, "as_taught_in": "Fall 2003", "language": "en", "departments": ["Electrical Engineering and Computer Science", "Mechanical Engineering", "Aeronautics and Astronautics"], "academic_levels": ["Graduate"], "instructors": ["Prof. Jacob White", "Prof. Jaime Peraire", "Prof. Luca Daniel", "Prof. Nicholas Hadjiconstantinou", "Prof. Anthony T. Patera"], "subject_primary": "Systems Engineering", "subject_secondary": ["Numerical Simulation"], "topic_tree": [{"name": "Engineering", "subtopics": [{"name": "Systems Engineering", "specialties": ["Numerical Simulation"]}]}], "topic_keywords": [], "pedagogy_tags": [], "assessment_types": [], "course_feature_tags": ["Lecture Notes"], "description": "6.336J is an introduction to computational techniques for the simulation of a large variety of engineering and physical systems. Applications are drawn from aerospace, mechanical, electrical, chemical and biological engineering, and materials science. Topics include: mathematical formulations; network problems; sparse direct and iterative matrix solution techniques; Newton methods for nonlinear problems; discretization methods for ordinary, time-periodic and partial differential equations, fast methods for partial differential and integral equations, techniques for dynamical system model reduction and approaches for molecular dynamics. This course was also taught as part of the Singapore-MIT Alliance (SMA) programme as course number SMA 5211 (Introduction to Numerical Simulation).", "course_url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-336j-introduction-to-numerical-simulation-sma-5211-f